\label{def:dartwalk}


Let $x, y \in V(G)$. A \emph{dartwalk} from $x$ to $y$ on $G$ is given by a finite list
$(d_1, \ldots, d_m)$ of darts, satisfying the following:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $\spt(d_1)=x$ and $\ept(d_m)=y$;
    \item for any $i \in \{1, \ldots, m-1\}$, $\ept(d_{i+1}) = \spt(d_i)$.
  \end{itemize}

\label{def:reachable}


We define a binary relation on $V(G)$ by: $x \sim y$ if there exists a dartwalk
from $x$ to $y$ on $G$. We then say $x$ and $y$ are \emph{reachable}.

\label{def:dartpath}

A dartwalk $(d_i)_{1 \leq i \leq m}$ is called a \emph{path} if
for all $1 \leq i,j \leq m$, $\spt(d_i) = \spt(d_j) \Rightarrow i=j$.

\label{def:tree}
The graph $G$ is a \emph{tree} if, for all $x$, $y \in V(G)$,
there exists a unique path from $x$ to $y$.

\label{lem:connected_iff_exists_forall_reachable}

The graph $G$ is connected if and only if there exists $x \in V(G)$ such that,
for all $y \in V(G)$, $x$ and $y$ are reachable.

\label{def:connected_component}

A \emph{connected component} of $G$ is an equivalence class of $V(G)$ for
the relation reachable.

\label{def:connected_component_Mk}

For any vertex $x$, we define $\ccm(x)$ to be the unique connected component
of $G$ containing $x$.

\label{def:covering_graph}

A graph $G_2$ is a \emph{covering graph} of a graph $G_1$ if there exists
a covering map $\pi : G_2 \rightarrow G_1$.

\label{def:rooted_graph_child}

Let $(G,r)$ be a rooted graph.
A \emph{child} of $x \in V(G)$ is an adjacent vertice which is not a parent.

\label{lem:exists_parent}

Let $(G,r)$ be a rooted graph. Assume $G$ is connected.
Any vertex $x \in V(G)$ distinct from the root admits a parent.

\label{prop:rooted_tree_unique_parent}

Let $(G,r)$ be a rooted tree.
